cmp1 = Comparison("iPhone (iOS) vs Android", [ios, andr])
ios = {name:"iPhone / iOS", attributes:["Swift + SwiftUI","Xcode — macOS only","App Store review gates every release","Higher revenue per user","Sandboxed: limited system access","Fast simulator on Apple Silicon"]}
andr = {name:"Android", attributes:["Kotlin + Jetpack Compose","Android Studio — macOS, Windows, Linux","Sideloading, alt stores, fast beta loops","Bigger global install base, more low-end devices","Deep access: adb, intents, background work","Emulator heavier, needs real-device testing"]}
tbl1 = Table("Toolchain at a glance", ["Aspect","iOS","Android"], [["Language","Swift / Obj-C","Kotlin / Java"],["UI framework","SwiftUI, UIKit","Compose, Views"],["IDE","Xcode","Android Studio"],["Host OS","macOS required","Any"],["Distribution","App Store only (mostly)","Play Store + sideload + alt stores"],["Review time","Days, strict","Hours, lighter"],["Test devices","Few models, consistent","Thousands of models/versions"]])
:::

For most solo or small-team devs: start on Android if you value iteration speed and zero hardware lock-in; start on iOS if your users pay for apps and you already own a Mac. If you target both, Kotlin Multiplatform or Flutter/React Native cuts the duplicate work — but neither removes the need for a Mac to build and ship the iOS side.

